The quickest way to get a feel for pancake is to watch one program call another.
So let us write the calling half, look at every line, and then point you at the
[surface]($link.page('surfaces')) that matches your language. The C, Rust,
TypeScript, Python, and Racket entry points sit just below the Zig walkthrough.

## In Zig

pancake is a [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) package, so the first step is to fetch it
into your build:

```sh
zig fetch --save git+https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake
```

```zig
const pancake = b.dependency("pancake", .{ .target = target, .optimize = optimize });
exe.root_module.addImport("pancake", pancake.module("pancake"));
```

The client itself is one object and a handful of verbs. Here is the entire
round-trip:

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const pancake = @import("pancake");
>
> // tcp: the peer is a place, a cleartext `host:port`. The slice names the
> // transports this client can reach; `tcp.at` spells the designator as a locator.
> var tcp_resident = pancake.net.tcp.Resident{ .io = io };
> const partner = pancake.net.tcp.at(designator);
>
> const conn = try pancake.driver.connect(allocator, &.{tcp_resident.netlayer()}, partner, .ephemeral, .{});
> defer conn.deinit();
>
> const greeter = try conn.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
> defer arena.deinit();
> const settled = try conn.call(greeter, .{
>     .verb = "greet",
>     .args = &.{.{ .string = "world" }},
> }, arena.allocator());
> switch (settled) {
>     .fulfilled => |arg| std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{arg.string}),
>     .broken => |reason| std.debug.print("the greeter refused: {s}\n", .{reason}),
>     // The third outcome: an introduction to a capability on another peer,
>     // which a `driver.Bridge` + Host redeems for you. This greeter never sends one.
>     .give => {},
> }
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```zig
> const std = @import("std");
> const pancake = @import("pancake");
> // In-repo builds only: `iroh` is wiring beside the package, not part of it.
> // From C and the bindings this transport is `libpancake_net_transport_iroh`.
> const iroh = @import("iroh");
>
> // iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. `.discovery` resolves
> // the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> var iroh_resident = try iroh.Resident.init(io, null, .discovery);
> defer iroh_resident.deinit();
> const partner = "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh";
>
> const conn = try pancake.driver.connectUriOwned(allocator, &.{iroh_resident.netlayer()}, partner, .ephemeral, .{});
> defer conn.deinit();
>
> const greeter = try conn.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(allocator);
> defer arena.deinit();
> const settled = try conn.call(greeter, .{
>     .verb = "greet",
>     .args = &.{.{ .string = "world" }},
> }, arena.allocator());
> switch (settled) {
>     .fulfilled => |arg| std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{arg.string}),
>     .broken => |reason| std.debug.print("the greeter refused: {s}\n", .{reason}),
>     // The third outcome: an introduction to a capability on another peer,
>     // which a `driver.Bridge` + Host redeems for you. This greeter never sends one.
>     .give => {},
> }
> ```

Read it as four moves:

1. **Connect.** `pancake.driver.connect` opens a session over a netlayer and
   hands you one owning `Session`; the iroh tab's `connectUriOwned` parses
   the locator string and calls it. A netlayer is pancake's word for a
   transport; the [Transports]($link.page('transports')) page introduces them.
   The `defer conn.deinit()` on the next line is the only connection cleanup the
   program needs; the arena's `deinit` reclaims the reply values.
2. **Fetch a capability.** `conn.fetch("greeter")` asks the partner for the object it
   published under the name `greeter`. What comes back is a capability, not a copy, so
   from here on you are calling the partner's object, not your own.
3. **Call it.** `conn.call` sends the `greet` method with one string argument. The
   arena you pass in owns whatever the reply allocates, so you free the result by
   dropping the arena rather than tracking each piece.
4. **Switch on the outcome.** A call settles one of three ways: `fulfilled` with a
   value, `broken` with a reason, or `give`, an introduction to a capability on
   another peer, which a multi-session CapTP Host + `driver.Bridge` redeems into a live
   reference for you. The compiler makes you handle every arm; this client meets
   a plain greeter, so its `give` arm is empty.

The tcp snippet is carried verbatim by
[`examples/zig/readme_quick_start.zig`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/readme_quick_start.zig),
which `zig build examples` compiles and runs, and `zig build meta-check` holds
this page to the example's lines, so the page cannot drift away from the
library. The iroh tab is the
[rooms client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/zig/client.zig)'s
shape: the endpoint bind gains a reachability posture, the locator shrinks to
the partner's bare node id, and fetch, call, and the settled switch do not
move. The id itself still arrives out of band, exactly like a host and port;
discovery turns a name you were given into a route, it does not browse for
peers.
The hosting half, the program that publishes `greeter` and answers the call, has the
same shape, and
[`examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig)
runs both halves together. From there the
[example chapters](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/README.md)
keep going down, from this application surface all the way to the raw engine, one
`step` at a time.

## From C, or any FFI host

The same library is also a plain C ABI, and that is the door for everyone not
writing Zig. It comes in two shapes. To dial, fetch, and call, use the owning
session: `pancake_tcp_session_connect` through `pancake_tcp_session_release`.
When your program owns the event loop, drive the sans-io peer instead:
`pancake_peer_recv_stream` in, `pancake_peer_next_event` and
`pancake_peer_take_outbound` out. Either way the surface is opaque handles,
fixed-width return codes, `ptr + len` views, callbacks, and explicit release,
the shape every binding wraps. Start with
[consuming pancake from C](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md)
for the build, the artifacts, the ownership rules, and a first complete program.

## In Rust, TypeScript, Python, or Racket

If you write one of these four, a binding already did the wrapping for you. Each one
is a genuine foreign-language consumer of the C ABI and presents an
idiomatic API on top, so you write the language you already know instead of foreign
function calls. Start at the [bindings overview]($link.page('bindings')) for the
shape they share, then jump to your language:
[Rust]($link.page('bindings/rust')),
[TypeScript]($link.page('bindings/ts')),
[Python]($link.page('bindings/python')), or
[Racket]($link.page('bindings/racket')).

## Then go deeper

Once a round-trip works, two paths open up. Read [the modules]($link.page('modules'))
to see how the library is layered and where each responsibility lives. Or read
[the concepts]($link.page('concepts')) first if capabilities, CapTP, and OCapN are
new to you and you want the ideas before the API.
